 {:db/id "q-216689" :question/id #uuid "804b67f2-c953-5690-bdac-d7da623929f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-216689" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $y\\in R$ such that $\\frac{y}{2}$ is not real." :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(y):\\frac{y}{2}\\text{ is real},\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∃y\\in R,\\,¬P(y).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃y\\in R,\\,¬P(y)) & ≡∀y\\in R,\\,¬¬P(y) \\\\  & ≡∀y\\in R,\\,P(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $∀y\\in R,\\,P(y)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $y\\in R,$ $\\frac{y}{2}$ is real." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-216689/ma-source-field/9f5ecc6c4725d5373bdcf7eb14af75c0a3c898fbaf5bf1af4ffc95450bcfd1cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
