 {:db/id "q-225023/ma-source-field/9a49f4c8d45b03f7f352065afd948a2a906346bc9f1b37125827ced764256f96" :answer-field/id #uuid "618ade45-7715-5c2b-8e23-5edac27f8883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-225023/ma-source-field/9a49f4c8d45b03f7f352065afd948a2a906346bc9f1b37125827ced764256f96/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-225023/ma-source-field/9a49f4c8d45b03f7f352065afd948a2a906346bc9f1b37125827ced764256f96/c990269799e88ca034500a3fd3413c3af18ea2ec1ebc05dddd4ad4a5ab508432" "q-225023/ma-source-field/9a49f4c8d45b03f7f352065afd948a2a906346bc9f1b37125827ced764256f96/7d9187d5c22ac5d5d33ef4b4bb6b14a4a6be6da74671d1a7062e7335f1de012b" "q-225023/ma-source-field/9a49f4c8d45b03f7f352065afd948a2a906346bc9f1b37125827ced764256f96/4153e51d0dadbc77137e2cf780b94e4ff3a779978668f53d711546d3bdd6f260" "q-225023/ma-source-field/9a49f4c8d45b03f7f352065afd948a2a906346bc9f1b37125827ced764256f96/b4ac6936776440214aaebffba73d7fd5fde48538e542fa8a6de8788032995d2c"] :answer-field/correct "q-225023/ma-source-field/9a49f4c8d45b03f7f352065afd948a2a906346bc9f1b37125827ced764256f96/7d9187d5c22ac5d5d33ef4b4bb6b14a4a6be6da74671d1a7062e7335f1de012b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-225023" :question/id #uuid "6706b3e3-ba1f-5e48-a0c6-87c0de600ccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-225023" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v(2-u),\\,y=u{v}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $1\\ge u\\ge 0$ and $v=1.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v(2-u),\\,u{v}^{3}) \\\\  & =(1(2-u),\\,u(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =(2-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-225023/ma-source-field/9a49f4c8d45b03f7f352065afd948a2a906346bc9f1b37125827ced764256f96"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
