 {:db/id "q-27482" :question/id #uuid "6948e752-4360-51fe-b06f-c6885f4f282f" :question/math-academy-id "q-27482" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c091fc5d742ddca3090725974f00ff7da77b1530d601bb82e288518b27970b0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.5.$ Therefore, $c=2.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-27482/ma-source-field/85109602addae07ed82d26dfe5776413894e25d794efd57c912491968aa88c39"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
