 {:db/id "q-30055" :question/id #uuid "85741874-6828-5db4-a1ab-e8bb9b524df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-30055" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-2x+1=1$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,pq\\,,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$ Thus, $pq=-1.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |x-1| & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-1=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-30055/ma-source-field/9f147844b82864d6658bb63e13b5ea9916827865297bd85441ba78fe44babfa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
