 {:db/id "q-31219" :question/id #uuid "98bca65a-5215-5c3c-9419-26f83fa4001a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31219" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-31219/ma-source-field/bb75d26efab5706c539f69a08d6a646c24a05ff345b65ee8d3eb5fccc1267b36"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
