 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "11a6774d-821b-5df3-83c4-601fbb258758"] :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (0,\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨1+{t}^{2},\\,-t+2,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in (0,\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(0,\\pi )$ if and only if each of \n its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (0,\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $(0,\\pi )$, we have that   $g(t)$ is also differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second component of this vector, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$, is undefined at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$,  we conclude that the function $h(t)$ is not differentiable on $(0,\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-32568/ma-source-field/bf41a4cf6f87b148582e5230a6b6876bfe9c134cb1d3c73a569ffb360e620618"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
