 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "10430503-ea48-51a6-b4f8-6c1fb5c683b2"] :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-32569/ma-source-field/24c442379d0075e135bedd6de7d069f1505f317741f5e9c93c13723e398355a0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
