 {:db/id "q-32608" :question/id #uuid "c7e3bf80-d71d-502c-8977-ed540b710cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32608" :question/problem "Given that  $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot D \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-32608/ma-source-field/ddc0a90b0887eb772e6d887fe8356de1cca6a30612dd1acb0e7276dccdab9709"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
