 {:db/id "q-332064" :question/id #uuid "f8dd5186-15e7-5cde-a17c-1b124d1cef2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332064" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{-9t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{-9t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332064/ma-source-field/191dd37483b1039dd922044162b4e44d7b364f53773870311d5d7dec13f63c71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
